{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1611 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ba08110989257612a6165fc9b8ef3d8092622adbf8f51418809cc18d2313f6f9" :edb/db-before-t 1610 :edb/db-after-t 1611 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534923 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:08:41.210Z" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192335 1100 "For the function  $g(x,y,z) = 2 (x + 1)e^z+ yz,$  evaluate  $\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial z}$ at $(1,1,1).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192335 1100 "For the function $g(x,y,z) = 2 (x + 1)e^z+ yz$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial z}$ at $(1,1,1)$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186192335 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂z} &= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x + 1)e^{z} + yz) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x + 1)e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 1)\\frac{∂}{∂z}(e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 1)e^{z} + y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂z} \\mid _{(1, 1, 1)} &= 2(1 + 1)e^{1} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4e + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 false] [17592186192335 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x+1){e}^{z}+yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x+1){e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & =2(x+1)\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & =2(x+1){e}^{z}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}{|}_{(1,1,1)} & =2(1+1){e}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =4e+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192336 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186192336 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}.$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192336 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y\\ln x) \\\\\n&= y \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}\\ln x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}\\ln x) &= y \\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln x)) \\\\\n&= y \\cdot ((2x) \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\\n&= y(2x\\ln x + x) \\\\\n&= xy(2\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 false] [17592186192336 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =y\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =y\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\  & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =xy(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192341 1100 "For the function $f(x,y) =\\ln(y-x^2)$, evaluate $f_x(1,9)$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186192341 1100 "For the function $f(x,y) =\\ln(y-x^2),$ evaluate $f_x(1,9).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192341 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (y - x^{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - x^{2}) &= \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot (y \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) - \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot (y \\cdot 0 - 2x) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{y - x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,9)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(1, 9) &= -\\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{9 - 1^{2}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 false] [17592186192341 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot 0-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,9)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,9) & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{9-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192345 1100 "For the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{4x-y}{x+y^2}$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(2,-1)$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186192345 1100 "For the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{4x-y}{x+y^2},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(2,-1).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192345 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4x - y}{x + y^{2}}) &= (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x - y) \\cdot (x + y^{2}) - (4x - y) \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2}))/((x + y^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot (x + y^{2}) - (4x - y) \\cdot 1)/((x + y^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4x + 4y^{2} - 4x + y)/((x + y^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4y^{2} + y)/((x + y^{2})^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\mid _{(2,-1)} &= (4(-1)^{2} + (-1))/((2 + (-1)^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 1}{3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 false] [17592186192345 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4x-y}{x+{y}^{2}}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2})}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot 1}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+4{y}^{2}-4x+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-1)} & =\\frac{4(-1)^{2}+(-1)}{{(2+{(-1)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186192609 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320192 13194139534923 true] [17592186192609 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320290 13194139534923 true] [17592186192609 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320297 13194139534923 true] [17592186192609 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320304 13194139534923 true] [17592186192610 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320248 13194139534923 true] [17592186192610 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320262 13194139534923 true] [17592186192610 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320269 13194139534923 true] [17592186192610 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320276 13194139534923 true] [17592186192615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320199 13194139534923 true] [17592186192615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320206 13194139534923 true] [17592186192615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320213 13194139534923 true] [17592186192615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320283 13194139534923 true] [17592186192619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320220 13194139534923 true] [17592186192619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320227 13194139534923 true] [17592186192619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320234 13194139534923 true] [17592186192619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320241 13194139534923 true] [17592186192619 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320255 13194139534923 true] [17592186193024 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534923 true] [17592186193024 1100 "For the function $f(x, y) = ye^{xy} - x^{2}y^{2}$, evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1, 1)$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186193024 1100 "For the function $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186193024 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}))-{x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot 1)-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}-2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,1)} & ={e}^{1\\cdot 1}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 1}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =e+e-2 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186193147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534923 true] [17592186193147 1100 "For the function $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y^{2} - \\ln (xz)$, evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1, 0, 1)$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186193147 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186193147 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-(0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2(1)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186193148 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534923 true] [17592186193148 1100 "For the function $f(x, y) = e^{xy} + x^{2}y$, evaluate $f_{y}(1, 0)$." 13194139534923 false] [17592186193148 1100 "For the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}y,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(1,0).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186193148 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+{x}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (1)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186320192 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320193 13194139534923 true] [17592186320192 1089 "q-132253" 13194139534923 true] [17592186320192 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534923 true] [17592186320192 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(3,-1,-2).$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186320192 1113 #uuid "adb985bd-dcb3-5be8-a084-1d0889512850" 13194139534923 true] [17592186320192 1145 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}{({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{-1/2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,-1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-1,-2)} & =\\frac{(3)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+4(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9+16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534923 true] [17592186320193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320194 13194139534923 true] [17592186320193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320195 13194139534923 true] [17592186320193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320196 13194139534923 true] [17592186320193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320197 1319